-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Training data
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The model weights were trained on the Hepatic Vessel Map (HVM) dataset:
  Paper: Xie T, Li X, Zhang L, Huang X, Liu Z, Huang C, Cai Q, Zhang Z, Wang C, Ma X, Huang R, Luo Z, Cheng G, Xu D, Liu Z,
  Lu C. Hepatic Vessel Map (HVM): an expert-annotated CT dataset for clinically applicable AI in liver vascular
  segmentation and surgical planning. Scientific Data. 2026;13:1259. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-026-07550-3
  Data:  Xie T. Hepatic Vessel Map (HVM) [Dataset]. Zenodo; 2025. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19885789
  License: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0), https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

How the HVM data were used (changes made):
  - Only the portal vein and hepatic vein annotations were used (the tumour annotations were not used).
  - Because the annotation file names do not follow the image file names, each annotation was paired with its image by
    image size, orientation and position (NIfTI header) and checked by the CT values inside the vessel masks.
    280 cases were paired one-to-one; 2 cases of Center 2 were excluded (ambiguous or no match).
  - The two masks were merged into one label map (0 background, 1 portal vein, 2 hepatic vein) on the image grid.
  - 224 cases (80 % of each centre, random seed 20261011) were used for training; 56 cases were held out for testing.
  - No HVM images or annotations are redistributed in this repository or with the weights.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party software and configuration
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
nnU-Net v2 (Apache License 2.0), Copyright Division of Medical Image Computing, German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ)
  Isensee F, Jaeger PF, Kohl SAA, Petersen J, Maier-Hein KH. nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep
  learning-based biomedical image segmentation. Nature Methods 18, 203-211 (2021). doi:10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z
  The model was trained with nnU-Net v2 (2.7.0) and is run with it.

TotalSegmentator (Apache License 2.0), Copyright Jakob Wasserthal and contributors
  Wasserthal J, Breit H-C, Meyer MT, et al. TotalSegmentator: Robust Segmentation of 104 Anatomic Structures in CT
  Images. Radiology: Artificial Intelligence 5(5), e230024 (2023). doi:10.1148/ryai.230024
  - The nnU-Net configuration (plans.json: network, patch size, target spacing and CT intensity normalisation values)
    is the configuration of the TotalSegmentator "liver_vessels" model (task 8), used unchanged except for its name.
    The CT normalisation values in it are statistics computed by nnU-Net from that model's training data; no
    TotalSegmentator weights were used to initialise or train this model (random initialisation).
  - At inference, TotalSegmentator's "total" models (task 298, 6 mm; optionally task 291, 1.5 mm) find the liver for
    the crop, and TotalSegmentator's prediction functions run the pipeline. These models are downloaded by
    TotalSegmentator under its own license; they are not part of this repository.
